{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–15 EUR), especially during downtrends; apply a downward level shift to the whole path when recent momentum is negative.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: after a peak, the model decays too slowly and predicts stabilization/rebound too early; enforce stronger mean reversion downward when the last 10–20 days show a clear decline.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: the model frequently projects sharp recoveries to the low/mid-80s that do not materialize (realized stays in high-60s/low-70s); cap upside drift when price is below its 20–30d average and trend is negative.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: the positive bias grows with forecast horizon (largest errors typically beyond ~10–15 days); increase the downward correction with lead time (e.g., small cut near-term, larger cut long-term).",
    "Volatility miscalibration: forecast path is too volatile upward (spikes) relative to realized during selloffs; dampen positive day-to-day changes and smooth local maxima following recent highs."
  ]
}
